1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,283
Price comes to a level and keeps getting stuck there.

2
00:00:09,734 --> 00:00:14,722
There is no line there on the chart, no support, no indicator.

3
00:00:15,173 --> 00:00:18,491
Yet price sticks there. Why?

4
00:00:18,941 --> 00:00:22,180
Because the most trading had happened at that price.

5
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:26,104
And you cannot see that from the volume bars at the bottom.

6
00:00:26,554 --> 00:00:29,793
For that, you have to look at volume turned sideways.

7
00:00:30,793 --> 00:00:35,705
Hello, this is episode seven of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

8
00:00:36,155 --> 00:00:40,439
Last time we understood the difference between real volume and tick volume.

9
00:00:40,889 --> 00:00:50,371
Three questions today. What is a volume profile? What are POC and Value Area? And where does price get stuck, and where does it run?

10
00:00:51,371 --> 00:00:54,297
A normal volume bar sits under the candle.

11
00:00:54,747 --> 00:00:58,221
It tells you: how much traded in this period of time.

12
00:00:58,671 --> 00:01:02,067
But it does not tell you at which price it traded.

13
00:01:02,517 --> 00:01:04,867
That is what a volume profile tells you.

14
00:01:05,318 --> 00:01:09,916
It counts volume by price, not by time.

15
00:01:10,916 --> 00:01:15,434
All day, tomatoes sold between 90 and 110 in the market.

16
00:01:15,884 --> 00:01:20,012
In the evening, count: how many kilos sold at each price.

17
00:01:20,461 --> 00:01:26,732
The most at 100, 500 kilos. A bit less at 95 and 105.

18
00:01:27,181 --> 00:01:30,499
Very little at 90 and 110.

19
00:01:30,949 --> 00:01:36,357
Write that count as bars next to each price. That is a volume profile.

20
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:39,550
On a chart it is built like this.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,207
Each candle's volume gets added next to its price.

22
00:01:44,657 --> 00:01:49,646
Where price stayed longer and more traded, the bar is long.

23
00:01:50,095 --> 00:01:53,804
Where price passed through quickly, the bar is short.

24
00:01:54,804 --> 00:02:01,413
The longest bar, the price where the most traded, is called the POC.

25
00:02:01,863 --> 00:02:05,024
POC stands for Point of Control.

26
00:02:05,475 --> 00:02:11,431
It is that day's fairest price. Where buyers and sellers agreed the most.

27
00:02:11,881 --> 00:02:17,027
Remember the fair price from episode 2? This is it, now with numbers.

28
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:26,308
The zone around the POC where roughly 70% of all volume traded is called the Value Area.

29
00:02:26,757 --> 00:02:32,322
Its top edge is the Value Area High, or VAH.

30
00:02:32,772 --> 00:02:38,179
Its bottom edge is the Value Area Low, or VAL.

31
00:02:38,629 --> 00:02:42,182
Inside the Value Area, the market finds the price fair.

32
00:02:42,632 --> 00:02:47,700
Above it price is seen as expensive, below it as cheap.

33
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:51,390
A profile has two kinds of areas.

34
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:57,326
Where the bars are long is a High Volume Node. HVN for short.

35
00:02:57,775 --> 00:03:01,903
A lot traded here. The market accepted this price.

36
00:03:02,352 --> 00:03:07,682
Where the bars are short is a Low Volume Node. LVN.

37
00:03:08,132 --> 00:03:12,338
The market did not stop here. This price was rejected.

38
00:03:13,338 --> 00:03:15,454
Now look at how price behaves.

39
00:03:15,904 --> 00:03:23,793
At an HVN price often slows down and gets stuck. Because both sides are willing to trade there.

40
00:03:24,243 --> 00:03:30,434
Through an LVN price often moves fast. Because there is little there to stop it.

41
00:03:30,884 --> 00:03:35,952
So think of an HVN as a speed breaker, and an LVN as an empty road.

42
00:03:36,952 --> 00:03:41,158
Remember the level from the start? It was the previous day's POC.

43
00:03:41,608 --> 00:03:46,519
There was no line on the chart. But the most people had traded there.

44
00:03:46,969 --> 00:03:50,208
That is why price kept stalling there.

45
00:03:51,208 --> 00:03:55,962
The shape of a profile tells a story too. Three shapes are the most common.

46
00:03:56,412 --> 00:04:01,400
First, the D shape. Fat in the middle, thin at the top and bottom.

47
00:04:01,851 --> 00:04:06,370
It means the market is in balance. Both sides agree.

48
00:04:06,821 --> 00:04:15,257
Second, the P shape. Fat on top, a thin stem below. Price ran up quickly and settled higher.

49
00:04:15,708 --> 00:04:24,538
Third, the small b shape. Fat at the bottom, a thin stem above. Price fell quickly and settled lower.

50
00:04:25,538 --> 00:04:29,978
When price leaves the Value Area, two scenarios are possible.

51
00:04:30,428 --> 00:04:37,978
One: price holds outside and new volume starts building there. It means value is moving to a new place.

52
00:04:38,428 --> 00:04:43,417
Two: price is rejected outside and comes back into the Value Area.

53
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:47,341
Then price often heads back toward the POC.

54
00:04:48,341 --> 00:04:50,457
There are several kinds of profile.

55
00:04:50,907 --> 00:04:54,538
Session profile: a separate profile for each day.

56
00:04:54,988 --> 00:05:01,597
Fixed range: you choose the start and end yourself. Like one whole range, or one swing.

57
00:05:02,047 --> 00:05:07,115
Visible range: a profile of whatever part of the chart is on screen.

58
00:05:07,565 --> 00:05:10,256
To begin with, stick to the session profile.

59
00:05:11,256 --> 00:05:13,685
Remember the point from episode 6.

60
00:05:14,135 --> 00:05:20,248
On spot forex and gold, the profile is built from tick volume. So it is an estimate.

61
00:05:20,698 --> 00:05:27,542
The real profile is on the futures chart. Where the two differ, trust the futures one.

62
00:05:28,542 --> 00:05:30,031
So, today's summary.

63
00:05:30,481 --> 00:05:37,795
A volume profile shows volume by price. The longest bar is the POC.

64
00:05:38,245 --> 00:05:47,153
The zone around the POC holding 70% of the volume is the Value Area. VAH on top, VAL below.

65
00:05:47,603 --> 00:05:56,197
Price gets stuck at HVNs and moves fast through LVNs. And the shape tells you whether the market is in balance.

66
00:05:57,197 --> 00:06:05,635
Next episode: VWAP. The day's average price, which big players use as their benchmark.

67
00:06:06,085 --> 00:06:10,917
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

68
00:06:11,367 --> 00:06:17,558
